   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the religious corporations law, in relation to electronic\nmeetings conducted partially or solely by means of electronic communication;\nand repealing certain provisions of such law relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S8663",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8663-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §28, amd §28, Rel Corp L",
    "lawSection": "Religious Corporations Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8663\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the religious corporations law, in relation to electron-\nic meetings conducted partially or solely by means of electronic commu-\nnication; and repealing certain provisions of such law relating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo clarify that religious corporations may hold meetings partially\nconducted by electronic means.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends section 28 of Religious Corporations Law\nto clarify that the Board of Trustees may elect to hold meetings\npartially by electronic communication, and provides that any meetings\nconducted partially or solely via electronic communication are subject\nto any guidelines or procedures that the Trustees may adopt.\n\nSection two repeals Section 28 of the Religious Corporations Law, as\namended by Chapter 98 of the laws of 2021, which has expired.\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSection 28 of the Religious Corporations Law as added to allow for reli-\ngious corporations to have the permanent ability to conduct meetings\nremotely via an electronic platform. However, the current language in\nSection 28, which states that the trustees may determine that a meeting\ncan be conducted solely by means of electronic communication, has been\ninterpreted by some religious entities to preclude their ability to hold\npartially remote (\"hybrid\") meetings where some participants meet in\nperson while others attend remotely.  By contrast, Not-for-Profit Corpo-\nration Law Section 603(a) contains similar language allowing not-for-\nprofits to hold meetings remotely, but explicitly states such meetings\ncan be held \"partially or solely\" by electronic communication. Amending\nSection 28 of the Religious Corporations Law to explicitly allow\npartially remote meetings will remove this uncertainty.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
